      "caption": "Daybreak, 21 October. The British fleet, standing north-east since it wore at 4 A.M., sights the Combined Fleet some ten or twelve miles to the eastward, standing south, with Cape Trafalgar just visible beyond them against the eastern sky.",
      "notes": "Mahan gives no clock time for daybreak; 06:00 is chosen because Nelson was already on deck with the enemy in sight and summoned Blackwood \"at about six o'clock\", and the first signals follow at 6.40. Positions are dead reckoning, not observation. The Combined Fleet centre is fixed from the one hard bearing available: Cadiz \"twenty miles to the northward and eastward\" of the allies at the moment Villeneuve decided to wear (about an hour later); working back from 36.533N 6.300W on a bearing of about 020 degrees puts the allied centre near 36.22N 6.43W, which also leaves Cape Trafalgar about 19 nmi to the east-south-east - consistent with the cape being visible only in the far distance. The British are placed 11 nmi west of that: Mahan says \"ten or twelve miles\" and Nelson's codicil says \"about ten miles\", so 11 splits the difference. Heading: Mahan says both that the British were \"standing northeast when the day broke\" and, generally, \"heading to the northward\"; 045 is used as the more specific of the two. Wind: Mahan reports \"light westerly breezes\" at midnight but west-northwest when the allied line formed; 292 degrees (WNW) is used throughout, since it is the direction he names for the battle itself. Strength for the weather column is 0.92 because the Africa had separated in the night and fought alone: whether the column is counted as thirteen sail including her or twelve, one of them was not with the body, and the fraction is the same either way.",
