      "caption": "6.40: form the order of sailing, which by Nelson's instructions is the order of battle; and prepare for battle. Ten minutes later, bear up. The two British columns steer east, about a mile apart, Nelson's to the northward - the weather line.",
      "notes": "Time is exact from Mahan (\"twenty minutes before seven\", \"Ten minutes later\"). The one-mile separation and the easterly course are stated; the columns are drawn a mile apart in latitude accordingly. The British have been advanced about 1.5 nmi north-east from the daybreak picture at roughly 1.8 knots, an estimate: Mahan gives no speed before the 1.5-knot figure attached to eleven o'clock, but the fleet was under all sail and the distances he does give imply something a little better than that early on. The intent arrows are the standing plan, not the ships' motion: the lee line on the enemy's rear, Nelson's line on their centre. The Combined Fleet still heads south here; Nelson noted the wear beginning at seven, ten minutes after this instant, so the arrow toward Cadiz shows Villeneuve's intention to keep the port under his lee before the manoeuvre starts.",
      "references": [
