      "caption": "The allies wear together to keep Cadiz under their lee. The scanty wind drags the manoeuvre out until near ten, and it inverts their order: the van becomes the rear, and Gravina, who led, is now the rearmost ship. Blackwood, with six miles still to run, begs Nelson to let another ship lead in.",
      "notes": "Time from Blackwood's \"about half-past nine\", and the six miles is his. That distance is measured here from the Victory at the head of the weather column - not from the column's centre point, which is drawn about 1.2 nmi astern of her - to the part of the allied line she was steering for. The Combined Fleet's heading is the awkward value in this phase: the wear began at seven and was not finished till near ten, so at 09.30 the ships are in every stage of turning. 350 is an authored average of those already round and heading north and those still coming about; no single heading is true of the body at this instant. The allied centre is carried only a little south of its 06.50 position, on the reasoning that the wear consumed the fleet's small progress. Note that Mahan's own distances - six miles at 09.30, three at eleven, a mile and a half at 12.10 - imply the British closed fastest in this stretch, about 2.6 knots, which sits oddly with his footnote of a one-and-a-half-knot breeze at eleven. The stated distances are followed rather than the footnote, because they are what he times the battle by.",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "mahan",
          "locator": "ch. XXIII, paragraph beginning \"When the development of the British movement was recognized by Villeneuve...\"",
          "quote": "When the development of the British movement was recognized by Villeneuve, he saw that fighting was inevitable; and, wishing to keep Cadiz, then twenty miles to the northward and eastward, under his lee, he ordered the combined fleets to wear together. The scanty wind which embarrassed the British impeded this manoeuvre also, so that it was not completed till near ten o'clock."
        },
        {
          "source": "mahan",
          "locator": "ch. XXIII, same paragraph, closing sentences",
          "quote": "The result of the allied movement was to invert their order. Their ships, which had been steering south, now all headed north; the van became the rear; Gravina, who had been leading the column, was in the rear ship; and it was upon this rear, but still the southern flank of the hostile array, that the weight of Collingwood's attack was to fall."
        },
