      "caption": "The allies carry sail to hold their places, and so edge continually nearer Cadiz. Nelson signals Collingwood: I intend to pass through the van of the enemy's line, to prevent him from getting into Cadiz - and the Victory's course goes a little to the northward. The allied line lies in a crescent, concave to the attack, five miles from horn to horn.",
      "notes": "Time is Mahan's \"towards eleven o'clock\" and the three miles is his inference from the Victory not coming under fire till 12.30. Again the distance is measured from the Victory, not the column centre. The weather column's heading is turned from 090 to 085 to carry the \"a little to the northward\" - Mahan gives no number, and a small change is all his phrase supports; the intent arrow to the north end of the allied line carries the rest of the meaning. The crescent's geometry is authored from two statements only: that it was concave to the British approach with the horns nearer, and that it was five miles horn to horn. The allied centre is bowed about 0.8 nmi east of the line joining the horns.",
