      "caption": "The Bucentaure strikes at five minutes past two, and at quarter past the Victory is shoved clear, head to the northward with scarcely steerage way. Villeneuve's last signal calls in his unengaged van; by half-past two they have their heads to the southward, five to leeward and five to windward, and at about three the windward five open a distant and ineffectual fire.",
      "notes": "Times exact. The Combined Fleet is set to broken here: Mahan describes the coherence of the hostile line as destroyed and the van acting on its own, too late and in disorder. Its heading of 190 is the van's, since that is the only part of the body still under way as a body - the rest are prizes and drifting wrecks, and no single heading fits them. Strength 0.61 comes from Hardy's report of \"twelve or fourteen\" in British possession; thirteen of thirty-three taken leaves twenty. The detachment arrow is the five van ships standing to windward - that is, to the westward, per Mahan's footnote - to the position from which they fired at three o'clock; the three o'clock cannonade is folded into this phase rather than given its own, since it changed nothing on the map but the position of that detachment.",
